+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to the production snapshot
+
+## Requirement
+
+`users.email` must be required (`NOT NULL`) and unique, comparing
+case-insensitively, without modifying or deleting any customer row.
+
+## What is impossible
+
+The production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`, owned by Support)
+already violates both constraints, so they cannot be enforced while every
+row is left exactly as it is:
+
+| Problem | Rows | Detail |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Duplicate email (case-insensitive) | 1, 23 | both `ana.silva@example.com` |
+| Duplicate email (case-insensitive) | 4 | `Ana.Silva@example.com` collides with rows 1 and 23 |
+| Missing email | 17 | `email IS NULL` (name: `Goran Okafor`) |
+
+A `UNIQUE` index by definition rejects a table whose existing rows are not
+distinct, and a `NOT NULL` constraint rejects an existing NULL. There is no
+SQLite mechanism to enforce either constraint while grandfathering
+offending rows (SQLite has no deferrable constraints across the lifetime of
+the data, and a partial index would simply not enforce the requirement for
+the excluded rows). Making the data valid therefore requires an update or a
+delete of customer rows -- exactly what this migration must not do, and
+exactly the work Support owns.
+
+Because of this, `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` cannot pass with any
+migration that actually enforces the constraint: `db.apply` raises on the
+duplicate/NULL rows.
+
+## What is delivered
+
+* `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` implements the intended constraint for a
+  database whose data is already valid (including a fresh database):
+  it rebuilds `users` with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and a
+  `UNIQUE (email COLLATE NOCASE)` index, copying `id`, `email` and `name`
+  unchanged.
+* The rebuild creates and fills the new table and the unique index before
+  dropping the old table, and runs inside `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`, so on a
+  snapshot with violations the migration aborts with the old `users` table
+  untouched (no rows modified or deleted).
+
+## What Support must do first (any of these, their call)
+
+1. Decide the canonical account for the `ana.silva@example.com` /
+   `Ana.Silva@example.com` collision (rows 1, 4, 23) and either merge the
+   records or give the affected row(s) a distinct, correct email.
+2. Supply an email for row 17 (`Goran Okafor`) or decide that the row should
+   not exist.
+
+Once the snapshot has no NULL and no case-insensitive duplicate emails,
+`002_unique_email.sql` can be applied as written and both checks in
+`test_migrations.py` will pass.
